      "id": 1,
      "text": "Stella began as Smith's Mills, a ferry landing where Joseph Smith operated a ferry, sawmill, grist mill, and tavern in 1742.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20,
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/stella_north_carolina",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The White Oak River, which borders Onslow County, served as Stella’s main waterway for commerce throughout its early history.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/stella_north_carolina",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1838, Matthew and Jemima Mason purchased about 1,000 acres from Stump Sound and established a family farm that anchored Stella’s growth.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/stella_north_carolina",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "After the Civil War, the arrival of railroads and the White Oak Lumber Corporation’s 1885 sawmill turned Stella into a bustling lumber hub for roughly 15 years.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9,
        15,
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/stella_north_carolina",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Early settlers in Stella were English, Scots, German, and French Huguenot families who came from northern colonies seeking farming and trade opportunities.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5,
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/stella_north_carolina",
      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Today, Stella supports local agriculture, fishing, and limited tourism, echoing Carteret County’s broader coastal heritage.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17
